    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d149">

                <pb facs="0328" n="238" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg238"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">651.113/101: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France (Edge) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-07-28T12:00:00+01:00">July 28,
                        1932—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received July 28—8:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>450. Your 265, July 27, 6 p.m. In conversation with M. Herriot last evening, reported in another telegram,<anchor
                        n="41" xml:id="d149-f1"/> I informally brought up the subject of the
                    Franco-Belgian agreement and its discrimination against the United States,
                    specifically mentioning the copper matter. The Prime Minister has asked me for a
                    memorandum outlining the situation, which is now being prepared.</p>
                <pb facs="0329" n="239" xml:id="pg239"/>
                <p>Replying to the last paragraph of the Department’s telegram under reference, it
                    is my understanding that France does not accord nations other than Belgium this
                    special 2 percent advantage covered by the Franco-Belgian treaty. I am further
                    informed that Switzerland has already put retaliatory measures in effect.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                    >Edge</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
